+### What the tower measures against
+
+The tower is compared with transformers running the same checkpoint, on a chart resized to 16×36 patches and 144
+merged rows. It lands inside the model's own bf16 noise rather than above it:
+
+| comparison | max | mean | cos |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| engine vs bf16 reference | 3.63 | 3.9e-03 | **0.99869** |
+| transformers bf16 vs transformers fp32 (the noise floor) | 3.86 | 4.2e-03 | 0.99784 |
+| engine vs fp32 | 1.64 | 2.7e-03 | 0.99952 |
+
+The engine is *closer* to an fp32 reference than the bf16 reference is, so what remains is accumulation order, not a
+port defect. All 27 blocks are checked the same way: engine rms tracks the reference within 0.2% at every block, and
+per-block cosine never falls below 0.9992 until the last at 0.99890. Nothing steps. The last block's absolute error
+rises only because the tower's own activations rise there (reference rms 1.5 → 186), and the engine follows that
+rise. The M-RoPE positions agree with `get_rope_index` on **513 of 513 cells** — all three axes, all 171 prompt rows,
+image rows and the text after them.
+
+### What it reads
+
+Asked for the labels on a chart of this model's own release benchmarks, it reads all seven:
+
+> AutomationBench, Terminal-Bench 2.1, NL2Repo, AIDA-Bench, BIRD, x²-Banking, GAIA
+
+The reference reads fewer and hedges. The one known limitation is narrower: asked to name *models* in a chart that
+has no model legend, the engine answers "SimShort and SimShort-Pro", which are not on it. The reference declines to
+name any. Reading text is correct; naming models a figure does not show is not.
+
+### With an image
+
+| metric | value |
+| --- | --- |
+| prompt tokens | 164 (144 image rows + 20 text) |
+| prefill | 1397–1519 tok/s |
+| TTFT | 0.11–0.12 s |
+| decode | 30.7 tok/s |
+
+Against the text-only numbers below, an image costs prefill in proportion to its rows and nothing per token.
+
+These image numbers were taken before the router and merge changes that brought text decode from 33 to 60 tok/s,
+and they have not been re-measured since.
+
+### When a port needs a dump
+
+Three environment variables write receipts beside the server, all off by default and each costing one environment
+read a prompt when unset: `TF_QWEN_VISION_DUMP=1` writes an image's merged rows to `vision-tower-<h>x<w>.bin`,
+`TF_QWEN_VISION_BLOCKS=1` writes each of the 27 blocks' residuals, and `TF_QWEN_ROPE_DUMP=1` writes the prompt's
+`(t, h, w)` planes with the ids beside them.
+
+One trap, hit while adding the last of those: the tower is built with `gpa.create` and assigned field by field, so a
+declared default on the struct never applies and any field not explicitly set is undefined. A dump field added that
+way segfaulted a served image request until it was initialised at load.
 
